                                                                                                Impact


If WordPress vulnerabilities are exploited, an attacker can:
🐚 Achieve remote code execution via vulnerable plugins/themes
🔐 Take over admin accounts
📂 Upload malicious files / web shells
🦠 Inject malware, SEO spam, redirects
🗄️ Steal user data, credentials, database contents
❌ Deface the website or cause service disruption
🌐 Use the site to attack visitors or other systems



                                                                                                Remediation

✅ 1. Keep Everything Updated
Update WordPress core
Update themes and plugins
Remove unused or abandoned plugins

✅ 2. Remove Vulnerable Components
Immediately disable or delete:
Plugins/themes flagged as vulnerable
End-of-life components

✅ 3. Enforce Strong Access Controls
Strong admin passwords
Enable 2FA
Limit admin users
Change default /wp-admin access rules

✅ 4. File & Server Hardening
Disable PHP execution in /uploads
Correct file permissions
Protect wp-config.php
Use .htaccess or server rules

✅ 5. Security Monitoring
Use a WAF
Monitor logs for suspicious activity
Regularly scan with WPScan

✅ 6. Backup & Recovery
Maintain regular backups
Test restore procedures


